  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight...Afternoon temps near to slightly above climo in \nthe U80s to around 90F. Peak heat indices in the L-M90s. Increasing \nmoisture across the region providing for considerable cloudiness \nover ECFL. Weak high pressure exists along the Eastern Seaboard. A \ndiffuse sea breeze has developed at the coast and will push inland \nthru the afternoon/early evening. Unsettled aloft with some \ntroughing as well as temps at 500 mb -6.5C to -7.5C. Expecting \ndiurnal rain chances this aftn/evening 25-50% - highest coverage \nsouth and across the interior. Primary storm impacts will be \nfrequent lightning strikes, gusty winds 40-50 mph locally, and \nheavy downpours. \n\nLater tonight...coverage across the Treasure Coast sneaks back up to \nSCT-NMRS (40-60%) as a slug of moisture is forecast to produce \nonshore-moving showers/storms. Further north, coverage along the \ncoast will be ISOLD-SCT (20-40%). Later shifts may feel the need\nto increase PoPs along the coast with further confidence or by\nmonitoring trends. With some of this activity across the Treasure\nCoast (late overnight/early Thu morning), we may have to watch \nfor some localized heavy rainfall and minor flooding if CAMs are \nto be believed. This activity may be low-topped but efficient \nwith high precip rates. Warm and humid conditions anticipated \novernight, with mins in the L-M70s. \n\nThu-Fri...Mid-level ridging centered over the southeast U.S. will \nslowly drift south/east into the western Atlc and off of the FL east \ncoast. At the surface, high pressure off the Carolina coast will get \nnudged into the western Atlc with assoc ridge axis remaining north \nof ECFL. E/SE winds will deepen in the low-levels and enhanced \neach afternoon at the surface with sea breeze formation and march\ninland. Moisture also increases further over the area (PWATs \n2.0-2.3\"). Temperatures warm a bit in the mid-levels but we will \nstill have occasional weak shortwave impulses traverse the area \nthat will aid convection (40-70%) - highest along the coast during\nthe morning, then diurnally across the interior. Primary storm \nhazards will be frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds, and \nlocally heavy downpours. A few ISOLD strong storms may occur.\n\nMax/min temps near/slightly above normal with highs in the\nU80s-L90s and lows in the L-U70s. Peak heat indices will approach\n98-103F. Highest low temp values at the coast.\n\nSat-Wed...Previous Modified...A mid-upper level ridge will build \nover the Florida peninsula this weekend and begin to break down \nearly next week with the approach of a trough. At the surface, a \nweakening cold front will move into the Deep South this weekend \nbefore shifting south into north Florida early next week and into \ncentral Florida late Mon into Tue, possibly retreating back north\nTue overnight/Wed. Locally, east to southeast surface winds will \ndominate through at least Sun, before winds become east to \nnortheast and increase a bit by Tue as the front moves through the\narea. Deep moisture (PWATs > 2\") will remain in place, which will\nenable PoPs to continue 50-70% areawide this weekend. As the \nfront nears, moisture will continue to deepen over the area early \nnext week, resulting in rain and storm chances (60-80%) rising\nfurther into early/mid next week. ISOLD strong storms will be \npossible with the front. High and low temperatures will continue \nto be slightly above normal thru Tue, but may get knocked back \ndown to near/slightly below normal behind the boundary.",
